Apple's <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/2026/10/28/apple-new-macbook-pro/">new touchscreen MacBook Pro models</a> start at &#36;2,899 for the 14-inch version and &#36;3,399 for the 16-inch version, but configuring the machines with every hardware upgrade pushes their prices well above &#36;14,000.<br/>
<br/>
<img src="https://images.macrumors.com/article-new/2026/10/macbook-pro-14-inch-and-16-inch.jpg" alt="" width="1600" height="900" class="aligncenter size-full wp-image-1074040" /><br/>
The starting configurations and U.S. prices are as follows:<br/>
<br/>
<ul><br/>
<li><strong>14-inch MacBook Pro with M5 Pro</strong>: &#36;2,899 with a 15-core CPU, 16-core GPU, 24GB of unified memory, and 1TB SSD, up from &#36;2,499.</li><br/>
<li><strong>16-inch MacBook Pro with M5 Pro</strong>: &#36;3,399 with an 18-core CPU, 20-core GPU, 24GB of unified memory, and 1TB SSD, up from &#36;2,999.</li><br/>
<li><strong>14-inch MacBook Pro with M5 Max</strong>: &#36;4,499 with an 18-core CPU, 32-core GPU, 36GB of unified memory, and 2TB SSD, up from &#36;4,099.</li><br/>
<li><strong>16-inch MacBook Pro with M5 Max</strong>: &#36;4,799 with an 18-core CPU, 32-core GPU, 36GB of unified memory, and 2TB SSD, up from &#36;4,399.</li><br/>
</ul><br/>
<br/>
The &#36;400 premium represents an increase of approximately 16% for the entry-level 14-inch M5 Pro configuration and 13% for its 16-inch counterpart. The M5 Max models see increases of approximately 10% and 9%, respectively. This is compared to the previous, non-touchscreen models' prices immediately before today's announcement, rather than their lower prices at launch before <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/2026/06/25/apple-just-increased-prices/">Apple's hikes</a>.<br/>
<br/>
At the other end of the range, <a href="https://www.apple.com/shop/buy-mac/macbook-pro-proled-xdr">Apple's configurator</a> allows the 14-inch touchscreen <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/roundup/macbook-pro/">MacBook Pro</a> to reach &#36;14,249 and the 16-inch model to reach &#36;14,549. Both configurations include the highest-end <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/2026/03/03/apple-debuts-m5-pro-and-m5-max-chips/">M5 Max chip</a> with an 18-core CPU and 40-core GPU, 128GB of unified memory, a 16TB SSD, and nano-texture glass.<br/>
<br/>
Storage accounts for much of the expense when configuring. On the highest-end M5 Max configuration, upgrading the standard 2TB SSD to 4TB costs &#36;1,000, 8TB costs &#36;3,000, and the new 16TB option costs &#36;7,000. Moving from 48GB to 128GB of unified memory adds another &#36;2,000, while nano-texture glass costs &#36;150.<br/>
<br/>
The <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/2026/03/03/apple-unveils-macbook-pro-with-m5-pro-and-m5-max-chips-with-neural-accelerators/">preceding 16-inch MacBook Pro</a> topped out at &#36;10,149 with 128GB of memory, an 8TB SSD, and nano-texture glass. The new model's &#36;14,549 maximum is &#36;4,400 higher, or approximately 43%, although that comparison includes twice as much storage. Configured with the same 8TB capacity, the new 16-inch model costs &#36;10,549, an increase of &#36;400.<br/>
<br/>
